@page "/docs/accessibility/overview"

@* The section's first page: the standard, how components are built for it, how it's checked, the gaps
   left (from artifacts/izzyblazor-a11y-inventory-2026-11-09.md), and the app's share. Until Nov 10,
   2026 this was the one article at docs/common/accessibility (redirected). *@

<ArticlePage Section="accessibility" Slug="overview">
    <Lead>
        IzzyBlazor targets WCAG 2.2 level AA and follows the ARIA Authoring Practices pattern for each kind of control.
        Components are built on the browser's own elements, so keyboard, focus and screen readers work the way the
        platform defines them. <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">IzzyBlazor's script</Anchor> adds ARIA only where it
        adds behavior.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Native elements first</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Buttons are <Code>button</Code> or <Code>a</Code>, dialogs are <Code>dialog</Code>, selects are
            <Code>select</Code>, disclosures are <Code>details</Code>, and checkboxes, radios and switches are real inputs.
            Enter, Space, Escape and the arrow keys do what the user expects, even with JavaScript off.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Where IzzyBlazor's script adds behavior, it adds the roles that go with it. Tabs are a radio group with
            JavaScript off. Once the script runs, they become ARIA tabs, and the arrow keys move focus between them. A dialog
            opened by the server becomes a real modal.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Each component's Overview ends with an Accessibility section. It lists the component's roles and keys, and what
            changes in each render mode.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">With JavaScript off</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Everything stays usable, but a few things are simpler. Screen readers announce tabs as radio buttons. A dialog
            opened by the server isn't modal. Keys that IzzyBlazor's script adds, such as a grid's arrow keys, don't work.
            Each component's Mode support table lists what changes.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">How it's checked</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>The test suite runs keyboard flows in Chromium, on static and interactive pages. Each flow opens, moves, chooses and closes, and checks where focus lands after each step.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>When a component is built or changed, its examples are checked in Chromium with axe and the accessibility tree.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>A sweep of every example checks that focus shows in Windows high-contrast mode and that targets are at least 24 by 24 pixels.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>When the page renders, IzzyBlazor calculates the shades of a brand or gray color you set to meet contrast ratios. The built-in themes are tuned to the same ratios.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit">izzy audit</Anchor> runs over every page of this site, at two widths, in light, dark and forced colors. Each finding is judged, and the last run had none that was real.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>A full pass with screen readers (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver and TalkBack) hasn't been done yet. Names, roles and states are checked in the accessibility tree, which is what screen readers read.</ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>
            A tool checks part of this, and a person the rest:
            <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/automated-and-manual">Automated and manual checks</Anchor> says which.
            <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/component-status">Component status</Anchor> lists what has been checked
            on each component.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Known gaps</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            These gaps fall short of WCAG 2.2 AA or come close to it. Each has a workaround today.
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="3">
            @foreach (var gap in Gaps)
            {
                <ListItem><strong>@gap.Gap</strong> @gap.Workaround</ListItem>
            }
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">What's left to your app</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Accessible components don't make a page accessible on their own. Your app is still responsible for these:
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>A <Code>lang</Code> on the <Code>html</Code> element, and a <Code>PageTitle</Code> on every page.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>One <Code>h1</Code> per page and headings in order. <Code>Heading</Code>'s <Code>Size</Code> changes the look, not the level.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Link and button text that makes sense on its own, not "Click here".</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Alt text that describes what a picture shows, and a unit in a field's label when it matters, as in "Price (USD)".</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Contrast for colors you set yourself, and time limits the user can extend.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Testing your pages with a keyboard and a screen reader: see <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/testing">Testing your app</Anchor>.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">In this section</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/keyboard-and-focus">Keyboard and focus</Anchor> explains where focus goes and lists every component's keys.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/names-and-announcements">Names and announcements</Anchor> covers labels, which components throw an error without a name, and what screen readers announce when something changes.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/color-and-motion">Color, contrast and motion</Anchor> explains how IzzyBlazor keeps contrast with your own colors and in high-contrast mode, and how it handles reduced motion.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/pointer-and-touch">Pointer and touch</Anchor> lists target sizes and the click-only alternative to each drag.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/component-status">Component status</Anchor> lists each component's keys, what the automated audit found and which screen readers it was tested with.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/testing">Testing your app</Anchor> explains how to check your own pages.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/automated-and-manual">Automated and manual checks</Anchor> draws the line between what a tool settles and what a person must check.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit">Audit a page</Anchor> and the pages after it cover <Code>izzy audit</Code>: verdicts, reports, fixes, builds and what it checks.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private sealed record KnownGap(string Gap, string Workaround);

    private static readonly KnownGap[] Gaps =
    [
        new("NumberField: the stacked step buttons are 16px tall at the default size, and smaller at the small size or Compact density.",
            "Typing the number works instead. To show full-height buttons on each side, set StepButtons=\"StepButtons.Split\"."),
        new("Field borders and switch tracks have about 2:1 contrast against the page. WCAG requires 3:1 when a border is the only thing that shows where a control is. Every field also has a visible label above it, so this is a judgment call, and some audits flag it.",
            "To meet a strict 3:1, set --ib-color-outline in your theme to a darker gray, such as #8e8e96 on white."),
        new("No full screen reader pass yet.",
            "Names, roles and states are checked in the accessibility tree. If a screen reader gets something wrong, report it as an issue."),
    ];
}
